218
00:35:06,627 --> 00:35:12,146
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay, so the final item in our business agenda is Budget update.

219
00:35:12,146 --> 00:37:28,405
[Dr. Richard Machesky] So we have, as we shared with the board, we have been watching, and we appreciate your patience as we've worked towards a, a more deep conversation around the budget and reductions and so forth. There were some important things that needed to take place, not the least of which was the finalization of the state budget, which we have now. So in conversation as a, as a team and working with Mr. Trudel, we thought it would be good to highlight kind of where we are now with, from a budget standpoint, with factoring in the actual numbers from the outcome of the budget, the state budget, and then as our board. Acutely aware, as is many of our community, there is a ballot initiative on August 4th that could have significant impact on our budget. That is the countywide enhancement millage. And although we don't know the outcome of that today, we'll know the outcome of that within the next couple of weeks. And so asked Mr. Trudel to kind of factor in what does our budget look like currently? What would that look like? And, and, and Keeping in mind the $12 million reduction that the board charged administration with finding. And then what would it look like without passage of the enhancement millage moving forward? As we think about reductions, the necessary reductions we would need to make in the '27-'28 and then ensuing '28-'29 school year. And then what about if it passed? What might that look like? So it provides the board and the community an idea of where we are short-term and then where we might be more long-term. So we thought that was a, a nice place to start, uh, to give us a basis for conversation and then, uh, an opportunity to, um, to get some input from the board, as was asked, regarding, uh, some of the proposed reductions. And then we'll take it from there.

220
00:37:28,405 --> 00:40:00,994
[Dan Trudel] So you have a printout of this here. This is on BoardDocs. We're also sharing it, obviously, so some of you can see it as well. So 3 scenarios at play. We'll kind of go through each one here pretty quickly, but at the top you'll see kind of our projected student FTE for each year. So the current year, '25-'26, that we just wrapped up, we were at 12,069. We're targeting around that 11,887 funded mark for the upcoming school year. And then thereafter, about a 200 FTE loss is what we've been seeing each year over the last couple of years. And then that's in line with the Plante Moran real point projections as well. So that's the basis for what those projected FTE numbers are. Foundation allowance. We had our $10,836 for the current year, and then the state of Michigan did bump that up. So all of the numbers here do reflect the revenue piece. Nothing has officially been sent to us, but based on the estimates that are being provided by the state of Michigan, those have been included in the '26 and '27 numbers as well. That way we have the most up-to-date information. From the budget side, we did come a little bit ahead from where we were from an original budget, which is good. The state of Michigan does provide some enrollment stabilization funding for districts that are losing FTE each year. Historically, that has been just looking back to the prior year. I respect that. And then you get a blend of the prior year and your current year, and then you get an additional pot of money. We've been getting around the $600,000 mark per year towards declining enrollment. The Senate pushed, and it went through into the final bill, a 3-year average of that now. So they're actually going back a few more years, and then they're blending that rate. So we went from $600,000 up to about $2 million in enrollment stabilization funding. So So about a $400,000 or $1.4 million increase there. And then there was a pretty large increase to 31a at-risk and our Section 41 bilingual funding as well for ELL students. So that was another $600,000 that was added. So what the state did there is they now have a weighted formula for at-risk and EL funding. They kind of went up to get it to where they believed it should be, and then going forward, it is supposed to get the same increase that the foundation gets then thereafter. So it was a pretty big jump this year coming up, and then after that it should be a pretty modest increase in line with the foundation as well.

221
00:40:00,994 --> 00:40:08,012
[Vital Anne (President)] So a EL student would get 120% of what a full-time regular FTE would get?

222
00:40:08,141 --> 00:40:09,137
[Rob Carson] Is that what you're saying?

223
00:40:09,137 --> 00:40:16,397
[Dan Trudel] It depends on the tier. There's like different WIDA tiers that they get, and that's how they dictate the funding based on the students that we report in those tiers.

224
00:40:16,750 --> 00:40:16,862
[Matt Haupt] Okay.

225
00:40:16,911 --> 00:40:20,652
[Dan Trudel] Yeah, so there's just a specific weight added to that. Category.

226
00:40:20,860 --> 00:40:21,101
[Matt Haupt] Okay.

227
00:40:21,325 --> 00:40:34,331
[Dan Trudel] And then based on the enrollment, it would then apply those dollars. So that was codified into law now. So every year going forward, the foundation went up 2.5% this year, that will be applied to that categorical in the future years as well.

228
00:40:34,331 --> 00:40:34,972
[Ayessa Potts] Okay.

229
00:40:34,972 --> 00:40:45,909
[Dan Trudel] So all that to say that we're about $2.1 million higher than original budget on the revenue side. So that has been factored into the revenue piece here in Scenario 1 for the '26-'27 school year.

230
00:40:45,990 --> 00:40:46,342
[Matt Haupt] school year.

231
00:40:46,438 --> 00:40:53,056
[Dan Trudel] So we were at about $191 million, so now we're at that $193 million mark for budgeted revenue.

232
00:40:53,056 --> 00:40:56,325
[Vital Anne (President)] So that brought up our fund balance slightly?

233
00:40:56,325 --> 00:41:01,933
[Dan Trudel] Yeah, it was around that 13.6% mark. Yeah, so we're just over 14 now, which is good.

234
00:41:01,933 --> 00:41:04,032
[Dr. Richard Machesky] So what was the—

235
00:41:04,032 --> 00:41:12,877
[Ayessa Potts] and I don't know where this might be on here, but the total savings that we had at the estimate of the severance incentive plan?

236
00:41:13,278 --> 00:41:16,708
[Dan Trudel] So that would just— it's integrated into that $12 million number.

237
00:41:16,708 --> 00:41:17,109
[Vital Anne (President)] Gotcha.

238
00:41:17,109 --> 00:43:14,749
[Dan Trudel] So you'll see in this Scenario 1, this assumes— this is what we've been using all along where there's no enhancement millage. And let's look out 3 years to see how do we have to restructure our expenditures. So you'll see nothing for the enhancement millage. And then under reductions, that $12 million is the number that was just embedded into the original budget. So that is based off our original budget there. And then in '27-'28 and then '28-'29, internally we have been looking at around $7 million in reductions over each year over the next 2 years after this school year. So that, that number is really largely driven by that fund balance percentage you see there on the bottom. So trying to slowly work our way back up to that 15% mark, obviously the only way to do that is to add back to fund balance. So We could cut more than that, but we wanted to find a number that we felt like would still be a sustainable number to actually hit in terms of that $7 million. But again, obviously, without an enhancement millage, you're looking at another $14 million over 2 years after this upcoming school year. So Scenario 2 looks at if we were to sustain that same reduction model over the next 3 years, so the $12 million this year, Coming up, and then $7 million each of the 2 years thereafter, but factoring in an enhancement millage. So if the enhancement millage passes for next year, it's approximately $8.1 million in the first year, and then that would go up typically by the rate of inflation each year. So that's factoring in a 2.9% increase for '27-'28 and then '28-'29. So I tried to highlight those in green just so you can see it broken out above our general revenue that we're currently getting. And then the reduction. So you can see there, if the enhancement millage passed, even with cutting $7 million in those last 2 years, you're going to be hovering around that 18% mark.

239
00:43:14,749 --> 00:43:15,759
[Ayessa Potts] Okay.

240
00:43:15,759 --> 00:43:59,584
[Dan Trudel] So Scenario 3, I said, okay, let's look at if we could reduce those future cuts from $7 million down to $3 million each year, assuming the enhancement millage passes. This is what your fund balance would look like then thereafter. So Getting us closer to that 15% mark. So, yes, the enhancement helps drastically, but we still have some restructuring to do then, even in those final 2 years, if we want to get that number back to that 15% mark. And then our goal is we do our audit typically in the month of August. So once we get our final numbers, we can plug those into the 25-26 column. And then that would then just update this model into the future years.

241
00:43:59,584 --> 00:44:10,175
[Vital Anne (President)] Is there any more insight into that $12 million and where we stand to what sections we're looking at?

242
00:44:10,175 --> 00:44:12,807
[Dan Trudel] In terms of like—

