00:44:55,010 --> 00:44:55,230
[Ayessa Potts] Okay.

248
00:44:55,230 --> 00:45:28,554
[Dan Trudel] We had a couple revoked, but grand total across all groups was just over 100. So that number is likely going to be closer to $2.5 million from the $1.5 million. So that's where we as a team have to look at that $2.5 million, just kind of shifts up to where the $1.5 million was, and then that leaves about $1 million remaining that we can look at that pot of money in terms of whether or not we can sustain certain positions or Knowing we still have to cut $14 million in future years, we could just leave it as is as well.

249
00:45:28,554 --> 00:45:29,726
[Dr. Richard Machesky] Okay.

250
00:45:30,208 --> 00:45:38,798
[Vital Anne (President)] I mean, I guess that's your thinking is you don't want to hire somebody who you need to let go next time. So you want to be very strategic in who you hire back, right?

251
00:45:38,798 --> 00:45:48,319
[Dan Trudel] Yes, because this— between the buyout and the $12 million we have, a lot of it was just simply through attrition. So as vacancies were occurring, we just didn't fill them.

252
00:45:50,710 --> 00:46:10,993
[Ayessa Potts] And taking into consideration also that even if the enhancement package were to pass, we're still looking at some significant cuts. So that's, I'm sure, part of your thoughts in terms of the staffing as well, right?

253
00:46:10,993 --> 00:46:37,124
[Dr. Richard Machesky] Yeah, exactly. I mean, the reality that we're working with Is, and this is what happens after an— after you do any type of severance, a buyout fee, is you should expect, and the company told us that you should expect for the next, at minimum, the next 2 years, you're not going to have any attrition. You're going to have most— they told us next year we should expect for 3.

254
00:46:37,124 --> 00:46:37,444
[Vital Anne (President)] Wow.

255
00:46:37,444 --> 00:47:08,574
[Dr. Richard Machesky] 3-ish, and then maybe 5 the next year. So very, very minimal. So any reductions moving forward are likely done through layoff. And we have been successful in— for the sake of the audience, we've been successful this go-round through the severance to not have to lay anyone off in any group.

256
00:47:08,574 --> 00:47:08,687
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay.

257
00:47:09,949 --> 00:48:59,364
[Dr. Richard Machesky] And so we were able to do that through moving, moving some folks around and so forth. So not everyone is where they may necessarily want to be, but everyone has a position. And we've not had to lay anyone off, and that would certainly be my recommendation moving forward. We not put ourselves in a position where we would have to lay someone off. That would be the worst thing that we could do. While also recognizing, and we, we see the other side of that, while also recognizing that we, we don't want— we don't want to cut ourselves too thin to the point where we can't provide services. We can't provide the supports necessary for students in our, in our community moving forward. So there's that, that needle, if you will, that we have to thread there to put ourselves in a position to do, to do what's right for programming, to do what's right for Mental wellness and safety and security and all of those things that we, that we value, uh, whilst also keeping in mind being fiscally prudent and, and not putting ourselves in a position where, where we're going to, um, you know, have to lay someone off moving forward. So that's the, that's the delicate dance that we're trying to maneuver through right now. Uh, we do know that there's some additional savings that we, that we have, to Dan's point. With the severance. The question then remains is, do we push that forward knowing that there are potentially other reductions? Or we wanted feedback from the board in terms of if we were to look at undoing, if you will, or slowing down some reduction, what might that look like?

258
00:48:59,364 --> 00:48:59,494
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay.

259
00:49:00,589 --> 00:49:01,839
[Dan Trudel] From the board's perspective.

260
00:49:01,839 --> 00:49:49,755
[Dr. Richard Machesky] And what we recognize that this, the timing on this was such that, you know, we had to work through this severance process and it did not allow board members a lot of opportunity to provide input. So we recognize that and value that input and want to be able to utilize that input moving forward. And also acknowledging that we've got a couple of weeks here, the ballot initiative on August 4th. You know, that, that potentially changes a significant amount of this conversation, right? It changes this conversation one way or the other fairly significantly. And we certainly want to give ourselves the time to, to be able to, to see the outcome of that and factor that into the decisions that we make that impact us at the start of school year.

261
00:49:49,755 --> 00:49:50,124
[Ayessa Potts] Thank you.

262
00:49:53,432 --> 00:49:54,677
[Vital Anne (President)] Any comments from you?

263
00:49:54,677 --> 00:50:09,848
[Ayessa Potts] So I think what I'm hearing is that for, for now we have to assume that we have to go on the budget as is. Like, we can't, we can't make decisions on money we don't have, right?

264
00:50:10,105 --> 00:50:17,601
[Vital Anne (President)] Um, and $7 million is a lot to cut in future years without laying off anybody.

265
00:50:17,957 --> 00:50:33,940
[Ayessa Potts] Well, I don't And tell me if this is incorrect, but in an organization where there's people are your main thing, 90%, I don't see a way in which you can reduce $7 million without affecting personnel. Am I wrong?

266
00:50:33,940 --> 00:50:36,260
