00:52:03,753 --> 00:52:04,747
[Vital Anne (President)] Oh, you're removing.

275
00:52:04,843 --> 00:52:05,019
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay.

276
00:52:05,083 --> 00:52:21,736
[Vital Anne (President)] So on the rest, we almost double Okay, so the potentially, if we keep that list the same, are we saying that would come to $13 million in savings and that potentially could reduce next year by a couple of million?

277
00:52:21,736 --> 00:52:26,078
[Dr. Richard Machesky] That would reduce that $7 million moving forward, right? Exactly.

278
00:52:26,078 --> 00:52:29,150
[Vital Anne (President)] Yeah, $7 million moving forward.

279
00:52:29,150 --> 00:52:29,942
[Matt Haupt] Yeah, correct.

280
00:52:31,640 --> 00:53:11,469
[Dr. Richard Machesky] And then whatever additional savings we find comes off of that makes that reduction even less impactful. It's the compounding effect that really, that really gets us. And I think that's the piece that our— blame our community at that— that's the piece that most folks just don't, don't understand. It's, it's the compounding from year to year that creates the challenge for us. Um, and yeah, that was also why it was important for us to, to, um, To know what our contractual agreements were moving forward so that we could have a budget for those and so forth more long-term.

281
00:53:11,469 --> 00:53:18,266
[Vital Anne (President)] I do have a—

282
00:53:18,347 --> 00:53:24,635
[Vital Anne (President)] I asked Dr. Machesky a question regarding special ed budget because this was a topic that we've talked about a little bit.

283
00:53:26,346 --> 00:53:33,861
[Vital Anne (President)] The fact that we're cutting a decent amount, or at least that proposal was cutting a decent amount in the special education as a percentage.

284
00:53:33,925 --> 00:53:34,775
[Dr. Richard Machesky] Sounds large, correct?

285
00:53:35,015 --> 00:53:40,624
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay, but I did want to get a historical perspective if there's— you can go over that.

286
00:53:40,624 --> 00:53:59,736
[Dr. Richard Machesky] And we were able to share some information with the, um, with the board, uh, prior, and we hadn't shared that necessarily, uh, publicly, but, but we can So the question was, where were we pre-pandemic in terms of some of the staffing levels versus where are we now post-pandemic?

