00:54:00,550 --> 00:54:00,986
[Matt Haupt] So special—

288
00:54:01,051 --> 00:54:18,102
[Dr. Richard Machesky] from a special education standpoint, uh, in, in between 2018 and today, or where we ended this year, we increased our costs, increased about $12 million. So our expenditures increased over that period of time about $12 million.

289
00:54:18,102 --> 00:54:18,442
[Matt Haupt] Okay.

290
00:54:18,956 --> 00:55:13,292
[Dr. Richard Machesky] And during that same period of time, we— the, the number of students identified with IEPs went up by 34 students. So the number of IEPs went up by 34. The expenditure itself went up $12,000 or $12 million, just a little over $12 million. So a lot of that had to do with the fact that during the pandemic we had those ESSER monies, uh, and we were directed to to spend those monies to support students, and that's exactly what we did. We directed those monies to help support students, and we added staff during that period of time. We, we just didn't right-size that staff over that same period. So when the monies were gone, we didn't immediately right-size staff. Our goal at that point was to spend that '23-'24, '24-'25, and then '25 into '26 through attrition.

291
00:55:13,292 --> 00:55:13,470
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay.

292
00:55:13,848 --> 00:57:13,373
[Dr. Richard Machesky] We would start reducing staff during that period of time, and we attempted to do that. We just didn't get the attrition savings over that period of time that we, that we wanted. So we couldn't bring down that staffing number over that period of time as quickly as we would have liked to have. So I would liken this to— this is a right-sizing from, from my perspective in terms of the number of students we're serving. Uh, in, in relation to the amount of resource we have to serve those students. So we're just— we're more rightsizing based on the revenue that's no longer available to us through the ESSER sum. And the key here is recognizing that any student that has an IEP will be serviced accordingly, according to their IEP. That's something we're obligated to do and will absolutely do. So any student that needs that will be serviced based on the staffing models that we put forward. So during that period of time, where did we add staffing? Well, we had social work staff during that period of time. We had some counseling staff during that period of time that went to help support students. We had an increase in the number of student support aides during that period of time. And quite frankly, we could, we could Afforded. That was an allowable expense through those dollars. So that's— we rationalize utilizing that. Again, as those dollars are no longer there, you'll see that those are some of the areas that we recommended some reductions in. Again, for the community to know that we did not eliminate any program or any service. We simply are reducing in some of those areas, tightening ratios and so forth, in an effort to just bring our staffing more in line.

293
00:57:13,373 --> 00:57:27,390
[Dan Trudel] In a handful of districts that I've worked with for the last couple of years, very common theme. Discussions are going on in those districts, same kind of deal, the right size to pre-pandemic level. Yeah, if it's any past that.

