323
01:00:19,849 --> 01:01:03,026
[Dan Trudel] So a lot of our grants have, particularly our Title grants, have supported conferences and professional development. Our focus going into this school year is to take a lot of those conferences and just put them on the back burner for a little while and then put in staffing into those grants in order to sustain the positions that we do have. So something like guidance counselors, we can utilize 31AA money as well as 31A at-risk funding for a portion of those guidance counselors. So we're already— we've been doing that for the last number of years. As there's been budget pressure on the general fund, we've tried to shift it over. And then likewise with social workers, our 31AA grant has really helped support those.

324
01:01:03,026 --> 01:01:03,205
[Stephanie Zendler (virtual)] Okay.

325
01:01:04,045 --> 01:01:27,781
[Dan Trudel] We do have some carryover into the upcoming school year that's been factored in, but we have been told that don't expect those dollars after this upcoming school year. So it seems like the state is going to start to pare back on those. A lot of those 31a monies were funded by ESSER funds that the state had. So those funds were essentially set aside and then they would pull on them over each year, and then that pot is starting to dry up on there.

326
01:01:27,781 --> 01:01:33,500
[Ayessa Potts] Yeah. With the new weighted formula, isn't that kind of like replacing them in a way?

327
01:01:33,500 --> 01:01:47,140
[Dan Trudel] More or less, yeah. So basically, you have— we probably have $2 million of 31AA expenditures. They gave us a $600,000 increase to at-risk funding, right? So we'll be able to keep some of those 31AA positions, but not all of them.

328
01:01:47,340 --> 01:02:59,145
[Dr. Richard Machesky] Yeah. Well, and from time to time I hear— well, um, I will hear, and it's appropriate, it's an appropriate question, why Why, why would you consider making a reduction here or keeping maintaining this and not that? And it goes directly to what, what Dan is mentioning right now. Because the state provides categoricals, they also have clear guardrails around how you— what can be spent within that categorical. So as the state provides categoricals, if it required— if we want to take advantage of that money, it requires us that we spend or that we're spending our resources within those areas. And so that's why in some cases we have to make choices. Why we're maintaining it in this particular group is because there's a categorical that supports that and doesn't allow us to insert a different group into that. And so it's another bit of the dance that we have to do with the state in terms of how they provide funding. And they're telling us they want us to They want us to prioritize funding in certain ways, correct? And we're required to do that. And so that's why—

329
01:02:59,145 --> 01:03:06,371
[Vital Anne (President)] Were we going to get more 31a funding this year because a lot of schools didn't take advantage of it?

330
01:03:06,371 --> 01:03:19,767
[Dan Trudel] That's a possibility. I would anticipate more will take it. About half took it last year. We budgeted as if everyone took it. And then obviously, once you are required to submit that, typically in October, we'll get the final number and then we'll amend it thereafter.

331
01:03:19,831 --> 01:03:23,196
[Vital Anne (President)] But So there may be some money coming from 31a?

332
01:03:23,196 --> 01:03:23,837
[Dan Trudel] Correct.

333
01:03:24,061 --> 01:03:24,334
[Matt Haupt] Yeah.

334
01:03:24,622 --> 01:04:01,632
[Dan Trudel] And then on the transportation side, we have Section 22L funding, which is for general ed student transportation. Historically, about 4 years ago, this started to provide money to districts for general ed transportation. We only would get special ed money prior to that. That was like a 4-year pot of money that this upcoming year is the last year of that pot of money. So if the state chooses not to renew any of those funds, for us, that's about $700,000 a year that we've been getting as an offset. So just other things we're keeping tabs on because there's a lot of games involved with some of these categoricals.

335
01:04:02,021 --> 01:04:04,387
[Dr. Richard Machesky] So, but that's our job to track.

336
01:04:04,387 --> 01:04:08,962
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay.

337
01:04:08,962 --> 01:04:11,585
[Vital Anne (President)] Um, any other questions on the budget?

338
01:04:13,645 --> 01:05:08,385
[Dr. Richard Machesky] No, I think that there are more to come. Uh, I, I might take away a plea, I guess, for, for the community would be please pay attention to the information that has been provided, uh, by the district, uh, around that ballot initiative on August 4th. Um, pay close attention to it because it will have significant impact on not just Troy School District but all districts across Oakland County moving forward because we're not We're not the only district in this position. Every one of my colleagues is sitting at this— at a table like this right about now, having some real challenging conversations around what they're prioritizing for the coming school year and the school year after. And also, I think really important to note is that these are difficult decisions to make, and it's not about— it's not a— we're not placing a value on any one over another.

339
01:05:08,385 --> 01:05:08,499
[Vital Anne (President)] Right.

340
01:05:09,059 --> 01:05:24,156
[Dr. Richard Machesky] It's, it's just in an effort to do the work that we need to do. There are some areas that need to be prioritized and others that, that will, will be looked at for reductions as we move forward. Yeah, these are hard decisions to make.

341
01:05:24,156 --> 01:06:27,580
[Ayessa Potts] Um, just a comment that I wanted to make, um, just kind of reading through the different concerns on social media. Um, one of the comments was, you know, that millage money coming back and supporting teacher staff and students. You know, obviously if we have more, you know, more money coming in per pupil, we can look at some of these proposed reductions, um, and make some decisions around that. But I think people aren't aware also that our, um, our 3 contracts with The TEA TESA and TESPA also include clauses that would increase their pay. So that is a solid in-contract commitment that those monies, that they would see a benefit from that. So I wanted to just bring that up because there was a concern that our staff would not receive a benefit from that.

342
01:06:27,580 --> 01:06:27,856
[Vital Anne (President)] Okay.

343
01:06:28,344 --> 01:06:33,990
